WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.040
INTRO:
Eversince the Gold Rush started in 1848,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:08.160
California has been the land of dreams. Whether&nbsp;
you were an immigrant looking for a better life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:14.240
or an aspiring entrepreneur that wanted to hit it&nbsp;
big, your best shot was in California. Over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.680
this led to California housing some of the biggest&nbsp;
companies in the world from Wells Fargo and Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.880
to Apple and Google. And consequently, we&nbsp;
also saw the wealthiest people in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.000
reside in fancy mansions across California.&nbsp;
Eventually, California’s economy became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.640
so large that it started dwarfing countries.&nbsp;
Right now, California’s GDP stands at $3.356&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:43.520
trillion. This makes California the 5th largest&nbsp;
economy in the world. But, despite this title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.520
it hasn’t been nearly that great for most&nbsp;
Californian citizens for quite some time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.400
In fact, most Californians are struggling to&nbsp;
make ends meet due to the rapidly growing wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.080
inequality in the state. Things have gotten so bad&nbsp;
that California actually become the state with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:04.240
highest poverty level in the US. This isn’t to&nbsp;
say that Californians aren’t making money per se,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.040
but rather that the cost of living in the state&nbsp;
is so high that even decent income earners fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.200
below the poverty line. According to the US&nbsp;
Census Bureau, a whopping 15.4% of Californians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.480
are living in poverty. And I’m sure there’s loads&nbsp;
of people that are just above the poverty line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.840
that don’t have it much better. So,&nbsp;
what happened to the Golden State?

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.560
OWN NOTHING &amp; BE HAPPY:&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.680
With sky high inflation and rapidly rising prices&nbsp;
across the country, we’ve started to hear this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.280
phrase more and more: You will own nothing and&nbsp;
be happy. While this may sound a bit cynical and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.720
pessimistic, this is exactly what’s been happening&nbsp;
in California for many decades. Californians have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.040
slowly been priced out of one thing after the&nbsp;
other which has forced them to rent or give up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.080
the given item. Likely the best example of&nbsp;
this is housing. Much of the US has seen a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.200
significant rise in housing prices eversince the&nbsp;
pandemic started. This has led many to hope for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.160
housing market crash so that they can finally buy&nbsp;
for a reasonable price. But, even if there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.200
housing crash, it wouldn’t be much easier for&nbsp;
Californians to buy a house. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:18.080
the median sale price across the entire state is a&nbsp;
whopping $843,000. Even if the housing market were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.520
to crash 30%, the median sale price would stand&nbsp;
at $600,000 which is still higher than median&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.640
sale price before the pandemic started. The median&nbsp;
household income in California currently stands at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.720
$78,000. This means that the average house costs&nbsp;
over 10 times the average family’s annual income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:41.120
Even with a 30% crash, the average house&nbsp;
would cost about 7.5 times the average income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.520
This has made home ownership completely&nbsp;
unattainable for the average Californian family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.240
and if you’re a single person, you probably&nbsp;
wouldn’t even consider a house. To make things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.280
worse, it’s not like things are much better for&nbsp;
renters. For families, renting a 3 bedroom house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.360
will on average cost them $3,000 per month or&nbsp;
$36,000 per year. That’s literally higher than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:07.520
the median personal income in the US in 2020. It’s&nbsp;
not just housing that’s crazy expensive either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.000
Basically every facet of daily life is&nbsp;
significantly more expensive. Take gym memberships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.360
for example. The national average for a gym&nbsp;
membership is $58. Meanwhile, the average gym&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:25.520
membership in LA is $70. Similarly, the average&nbsp;
haircut for women is $69 and the average haircut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:32.480
for men is $45 in California. The national average&nbsp;
is $44 for women and $28 for men. These insane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.560
Californian prices carry over to clothing as well.&nbsp;
The average pair of jeans cost $60 in California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:43.840
while the national average is nearly half of that&nbsp;
at $34. And I don’t even think we need to discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.960
gas prices which currently stand at over $6 per&nbsp;
gallon. Aside from getting screwed over from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.480
cost of living perspective, many Californians also&nbsp;
get screwed over from a tax perspective as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.280
It’s no secret that California has some hefty&nbsp;
state income taxes which range from 1% to 12.3%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.240
By the time the average Californian pays&nbsp;
their taxes and daily living expenses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.960
they’re left with virtually no money. This is why&nbsp;
living paycheck to paycheck is extremely common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.800
in California. In fact, the only state in which&nbsp;
it’s even more common is Hawaii. Considering this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.120
its basically impossible for the average&nbsp;
Californian to build any sort of wealth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.960
So, it’s quite ironic that most people are&nbsp;
only now complaining that they are forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.840
to own nothing and be happy. Californians have&nbsp;
already been experiencing this for a decade.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.560
FLEEING WEALTH:&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.960
Aside from barely making ends meet right&nbsp;
now, California is also posed to run into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.840
an opportunity crisis within the coming decades.&nbsp;
Here’s the thing, droves of wealthy companies and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.800
individuals are ditching California. Tesla and&nbsp;
Oracle, for example, have outright moved their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.680
headquarters from California to Texas. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
other companies haven’t quite moved their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.320
headquarters, but they’re investing significant&nbsp;
amounts of money outside California. Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.920
for instance, recently built a $1 billion campus&nbsp;
in Austin. Historically, this was something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.400
companies were extremely reluctant to do because&nbsp;
California simply had the best talent and the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:11.440
motivated people in the country. But, this is&nbsp;
no longer true. For starters, the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.160
country has caught up significantly thanks to the&nbsp;
opportunities created by technology. And secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.840
many of the talented engineers and white collars&nbsp;
workers in California have been fleeing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.360
state themselves. So, companies are no longer&nbsp;
limited to recruiting primarily from California.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.600
Aside from being able to recruit more diverse&nbsp;
talent, companies are also able to significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:33.680
reduce their payroll by moving to lower cost&nbsp;
states. You see, given the insane living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.560
expenses in California, companies also have to&nbsp;
pay insane salaries to hire the best talent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:43.360
At Facebook, for example, software engineers&nbsp;
at Facebook earn a minimum of $184,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.280
and this goes up to $1.61 million. This is the&nbsp;
same case with Google and every other mega cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.480
company as well. By moving to states like Idaho&nbsp;
and Texas, however, these same companies can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.520
get away with paying 20 to 30% less for the same&nbsp;
jobs. At first glance, it might sound like these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.760
companies are taking advantage of tech workers&nbsp;
from these states by paying them less, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:08.400
isn’t the case either. You see, these discounted&nbsp;
salaries stretch further in these low cost states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:14.560
than the full salaries do in California, so it’s&nbsp;
a win win scenario. Employees earn relatively more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.040
while companies pay less. On top of this, many&nbsp;
of these states are offering these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.920
loads of incentives to move to Texas. For&nbsp;
example, Texas offered Tesla up to an 80%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.880
property tax rebate for 10 years if they expanded&nbsp;
to Texas. So, it makes more sense than ever for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.680
these companies to leave California. And as more&nbsp;
of them make the decision to move, we’re slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.840
gonna see opportunity leaving the state which&nbsp;
will just make it even harder for Californians.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.920
EASY TO BE HOMELESS:&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.000
On top of high living expenses and fleeing wealth,&nbsp;
something else that has been heavily weighing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:51.600
on California is the ease of being homeless. Now,&nbsp;
I’m not trying to say that being homeless is easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:57.280
because it’s obviously not. But, with that being&nbsp;
said, California has the least severe consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.680
for being homeless. They have loads of generous&nbsp;
homeless programs and they even hand out money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.040
to homeless people. This man in San Francisco, for&nbsp;
example, is earning $620 per month by just living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.680
on the streets. Of course, this is not enough&nbsp;
money to live a royal life or anything, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.640
it’s also more than enough money to buy food and&nbsp;
basic necessities. Now, many people would describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:22.160
this as a benefit, and it somewhat is. While many&nbsp;
Californians aren’t exactly swimming in wealth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.720
they never have to worry about starving either.&nbsp;
Worst case scenario, they can live on the streets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.360
on checks from the government. But, at the&nbsp;
same time, this is also kind of a negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:36.160
as well. If you’re able to carry on basic life&nbsp;
by doing nothing, a good amount of people will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:40.400
choose to do nothing. For example, take a&nbsp;
listen to want this homeless man thinks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.880
And he’s not the only one who thinks like this.&nbsp;
California’s homeless programs are so generous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:17.280
that homeless people from other states immigrate&nbsp;
to California to be homeless there instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.520
Somehow, California has transitioned from being&nbsp;
the dream destination for entrepreneurs to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.080
the dream destination for the homeless. Not only&nbsp;
does this strain the resources of California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.800
but it also makes everyday life for Californians&nbsp;
way harder. Due to the large number of homeless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:36.320
people, it’s common to find human feces and drug&nbsp;
needles across their cities. In fact, this issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:41.920
has become so prevalent that San Franciso pays&nbsp;
people $184,000 per year to clean their sidewalks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.720
When you have to do this, you clearly have&nbsp;
a problem. And I don’t know about you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.280
but I wouldn’t want to go to work or have my&nbsp;
kids to go school in such a area. Leaving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.520
state would be one of the top priorities for&nbsp;
anyone with any sort of financial stability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:59.760
And as these middle class, upper middle class,&nbsp;
and high income families leave the state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:03.040
the state is simply driven further&nbsp;
into the depths of poverty.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:07.520
STATE OF CALIFORNIA:&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:11.440
Given the circumstances in California, I don’t&nbsp;
think you’d be surprised to hear that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:17.120
population is on the decline, but it’s not as&nbsp;
drastic as you might think. And this brings up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:22.240
the question: Why do people choose to stay in&nbsp;
California despite all these drawback? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.480
there’s a few reasons for this starting with&nbsp;
wealth bubbles. Most of the celebrities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.080
billionaires who live in California live in an&nbsp;
extremely niche portion of the state that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:34.880
filled with other wealthy individuals. So, they’re&nbsp;
essentially able to avoid all of the poverty and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:40.160
despair that average Californians face on a daily&nbsp;
basis. But, what about the average Californian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:45.360
why do they choose to stay. Well, usually, the&nbsp;
top answer is some sort of emotional connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.880
to the state. Maybe they were born and raised in&nbsp;
California and their entire family lives there. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.360
maybe, the person is heavily involved with outdoor&nbsp;
activities and good weather along with beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:00.160
nature are top priorities. Aside from some sort&nbsp;
of emotional connection with the state, many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:05.200
people choose to stay in California or even move&nbsp;
to California in order to minimize discrimination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.600
California is by far the most accepting place&nbsp;
in the entire world whether we’re talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.960
people of color or LGBT. So, people that are part&nbsp;
of these communities are reluctant to leave for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:20.080
obvious reasons. It should also be mentioned that&nbsp;
California is a sanctuary state which means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:24.640
they have some of the loosest rules when it comes&nbsp;
to illegal immigrants. In fact, California is home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:29.520
to over 2 million unauthorized immigrants, and I&nbsp;
don’t think these guys have any plans of moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.720
out anytime soon. Also, something else to note is&nbsp;
that, there’s like loads of Californians who do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:39.760
want to leave but their circumstances prevent it.&nbsp;
For example, they might have a unique job that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:44.960
only available in California or maybe they don’t&nbsp;
have enough money to take the risk of completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:49.840
uprooting their lives and moving to another&nbsp;
state. But, even with all these driving factors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:55.360
California’s population is still declining. So&nbsp;
clearly, the motivation to leave is quite strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:01.360
for many. At the end of the day, California is by&nbsp;
no means perfect. They have an extremely high cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:05.920
of living, high taxes, a massive homeless&nbsp;
problem, and companies are fleeing as fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:10.960
as possible. All of these factors have led to&nbsp;
California becoming the poorest state in America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:16.320
And from an objective perspective, it makes&nbsp;
little sense to stay. But, for many, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:22.160
not the only consideration. For them, California&nbsp;
is their home and though it may not be perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:25.760
they would rather stick with California and&nbsp;
try to fix the state than flee and watch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:30.960
state rot from affar. Would you guys stay in&nbsp;
California? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:34.640
drop a like if you hope that California can&nbsp;
make a recovery for the sake of its people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

